  "query_preview": "/Monday through Friday/...\nAnother upper-level shortwave trough will swing through the \nPacific Northwest on Monday, continuing the trend of suppressing\nthe Four Corners ridge. Flow aloft will be shifting from the \nsouthwest to out of the west/northwest, keeping shower and \nthunderstorm chances to the higher terrain of Eastern Oregon and\nSouthwest Idaho. PWATs will stay above 50th percentile through \nthis shortwave moving through, leading to localized QPF \nbullseyes with these thunderstorms, but will diminish Tuesday \nunder drier west/northwesterly flow. Slightly below normal / \nnear-normal temperatures will continue Monday and Tuesday as the\ntrough makes its way east through the Northern Rockies. \nStarting late Tuesday, a trend of the upper-level ridge \namplifying over the Great Basin will begin, including the SW \nID/SE OR region. The extent to which it intensifies is \nuncertain, however, temperatures will begin to warm Wednesday \nonwards. While this is happening Wednesday, the upper-level \nlongwave trough that has been sitting offshore slinging \nshortwaves through the area looks to finally move onshore to end\nthe work week. Again, timing is uncertain, however increased \nprecipitation chances and wind gusts from southerly flow aloft \nare expected.",
